<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#nutmeg">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nutmeg</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nutmeg is a spice obtained from the seed of a tree native to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banda-islands">Banda Islands</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maluku-islands">Moluccas</a> of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>. It was among the most valuable commodities of the early modern spice trade. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portuguese-empire">Portuguese Empire</a> profited from the spice trade in the sixteenth century, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-empire">Dutch Empire</a> later enforced a monopoly through violence, including the near-extermination of the Banda Islanders in 1621.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nutmeg.jpg" alt="Nutmeg" width="320" height="426" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Franz Eugen Köhler, Köhler's Medizinal-Pflanzen · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Myristica_fragrans_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-097.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The spice monopolies enforced across the Maluku Islands, covering <b>nutmeg</b>, cloves and mace, were maintained through deliberate violence, including the Dutch conquest of the Banda Islands under Jan Pieterszoon Coen in 1621, with the near-extermination and enslavement of their population.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dutch-empire">Dutch Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Its function was collection and redistribution: cloves, <b>nutmeg</b> and mace from the Maluku Islands; cotton textiles from Gujarat, the Coromandel and Malabar coasts and Bengal; porcelain, silk and iron from China; camphor, sandalwood, spices, fish roe and seaweed from the archipelago.</blockquote><a href="/empire/malacca-sultanate">Malacca Sultanate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Treaty of Breda of 1667 ended the Second Anglo-Dutch War; the Dutch did not press their claim, keeping Suriname and the <b>nutmeg</b> island of Run instead.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-netherland">New Netherland · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The spice trade was enormously profitable in the decades around 1500–1550, as pepper, cinnamon, cloves and <b>nutmeg</b> generated revenues that made Lisbon one of Europe’s wealthiest cities and funded the Manueline building programme, including the Jerónimos Monastery and the Tower of Belém.</blockquote><a href="/empire/portuguese-empire">Portuguese Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 19th century <b>nutmeg</b> and cacao replaced sugar, giving Grenada its spice reputation, and from 1885 St George's housed the governor of the British Windward Islands.</blockquote><a href="/state/colony-of-grenada">Colony of Grenada</a></li><li><blockquote>The Company enforced monopolies on <b>nutmeg</b>, cloves and pepper, used slave labour and forced deliveries, and intervened in the succession wars of the Mataram Sultanate, gaining much of Java.</blockquote><a href="/state/dutch-east-india-company-territories">Dutch East India Company territories</a></li></ul></section>
